WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:30.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. Recently, I've had the great privilege of traveling abroad. I've been to Italy and Spain, Japan a couple of times, England, Scotland. So I've been pulling out my American passport a lot lately. And every time I present it to a customs official, I always get nervous. I honestly think it's meant to make you nervous. There is something inherently fraught and maybe even a little ugly about the ritual of presenting your passport.
这里是 99% Invisible。我是罗曼·马斯。最近，我有幸去国外旅行。我去过意大利和西班牙、去过好几次日本、还有英国和苏格兰。所以我最近经常掏出我的美国护照。每次我把它递给海关官员时，我总是会感到紧张。老实说，我觉得它就是为了让你感到紧张而设计的。展示护照这种仪式本身就带有某种令人不安、甚至可能有点难堪的意味。

2
00:00:30.000 --> 00:01:25.000
And that this little booklet you're carrying around with you, whether it says you're from the United States or Japan or Haiti, confers so much power. I can't stand the idea that your value as a person could be determined by the fact of where you were born. But the minute you're asked to show your passport, it's obvious that's how the world works. Especially when billions of people live in countries whose passports grant them little to know meaningful access to the rest of the world. While an estimated 850 million people do not even have the documents necessary to prove their nationality or legal existence, which is why we wanted to share this story from the podcast, Far From Home, by frequent 99PI contributor Scott Gurian. A while back, Scott sat down with someone who deliberately surrendered his official nationality, including his passport, and began issuing what he believed was a better form of identification.
你随身携带的这个小册子，无论上面写着你来自美国、日本还是海地，都赋予了巨大的权力。我无法忍受这样一个想法：你作为人的价值竟然可以由你的出生地来决定。但只要你被要求出示护照，世界就是这样运转的，这一点就显露无疑。特别是当数十亿人生活在这样的国家里：他们的护照无法让他们获得任何前往世界其他地方的有意义的许可。而据估计，有 8.5 亿人甚至连证明自己国籍或合法存在的证件都没有。这就是为什么我们想分享来自播客节目《远离家乡》（Far From Home）的故事，该节目由 99PI 的常驻撰稿人斯科特·古里安（Scott Gurian）制作。不久前，斯科特采访了一位刻意放弃自己官方国籍（包括护照）的人，并开始发行他认为更好的身份证明形式。

3
00:01:25.000 --> 00:02:08.000
We'll let Scott take it from here. Back in October of 2024, on the eve of the presidential election, Donald Trump held a giant political rally at Madison Square Garden in New York City. And one of the warm-up speakers was his soon-to-be political advisor, Stephen Miller. In a fiery speech, Miller expressed what would soon become the throughline of the country's new anti-immigration policy. The rise of people like Stephen Miller shows that we're living in an era when nationhood and nationhood and. and national identity seem to matter more than ever. But it's kind of strange, because at the same time that fascists in America and elsewhere
我们把接下来的时间交给斯科特。早在 2024 年 10 月，即总统大选前夕，唐纳德·特朗普在纽约市麦迪逊广场花园举办了一场大型政治集会。其中一位暖场发言人是他即将上任的政治顾问斯蒂芬·米勒。米勒在慷慨激昂的演讲中，表达了很快将成为该国新反移民政策核心主旨的内容。像斯蒂芬·米勒这样的人的崛起表明，我们正生活在一个国家地位和国家身份似乎比以往任何时候都更加重要的时代。但这有点奇怪，因为与此同时，美国和其他地方的法西斯分子

4
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:30.000
are trying to get us all to think in terms of nations, we're dealing with all these global problems like pandemics, climate change, and AI. Meanwhile, technology in the 21st century often makes it easy to ignore things like nationality and national boundaries altogether. Therefore, I know this might sound sort of radical, but if it ever made,
正试图让我们所有人都以国家为单位来思考，而我们却在应对所有这些全球性问题，比如大流行病、气候变化和人工智能。与此同时，21世纪的技术往往让人很容易完全忽视国籍和国境之类的东西。因此，我知道这听起来可能有点激进，但如果它曾经成真过，

5
00:02:30.000 --> 00:03:11.000
made any sense, the entire concept of the nation state can sometimes seem kind of quaint and outdated for the modern world. This is the story of one man who reached all these conclusions himself more than 75 years ago, way ahead of his time. His name was Gary Davis, and I went to interview him back in 2009 when he was 88 years old. According to my directions, Gary's house was in South Burlington, Vermont, in the United States of America, but, you know, but But as soon as I pulled into his driveway, I noticed a sign next to his front door, proclaiming it sovereign world territory. It's good to meet you. Sorry, I'm running a few minutes behind here.
曾经有过任何意义的话，那么民族国家这一整个概念，对现代世界而言有时显得有些古怪和过时。这是一个人的故事，他在 75 年前就自己得出了所有这些结论，远远超出了他的时代。他的名字叫加里·戴维斯（Gary Davis）。我在 2009 年去采访他时，他已经 88 岁了。根据我得到的路线指引，加里的房子位于美利坚合众国的佛蒙特州南伯灵顿，但是，你知道，但就在我把车驶入他的车道时，我注意到他前门旁边的牌子，上面宣称这里是主权世界领土。很高兴见到你。抱歉，我这里稍微耽误了几分钟。

6
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:48.000
He came up to me, pointed out that I was standing on planet Earth, and asked me to present my world citizen card. I didn't have one, so thankfully he decided to waive the requirement. Then he led me into his living room where we'd sit for the next five hours, talking about a choice he made when he was 26 years old. in 1948. It was a simple act that took just a minute to perform, but it would make the rest of his life really complicated. Over the next six decades, he'd be detained and imprisoned countless times, dragged off the floor of the United Nations General Assembly, and gained millions of followers,
他朝我走来，指出我正站在地球上，并要求我出示世界公民证。我没有这个证件，所以谢天谢地，他决定免除这一要求。接着，他带我进了他的客厅，我们在那里坐了整整五个小时，谈论他在 26 岁时做出的一个决定，那是在 1948 年。这是一个只需一分钟就能完成的简单举动，但它却让他的余生变得无比复杂。在接下来的六十年里，他无数次被拘留和关押，从联合国大会的会场上被强行拖走，并获得了数百万的追随者。

7
00:03:48.000 --> 00:04:32.000
all while confronting bureaucrats who had absolutely no idea what to do with him. What set this chain of events into motion was Gary's decision to stand before a guy in a gray suit at the U.S. Embassy in Paris, put his hand on a Bible and read a couple of lines off a sheet of paper. I hereby swear that I desire to make a formal renunciation of my American nationality, and pursuant thereto, hereby absolutely and entirely renounce my nationality in the United States and all rights and privileges thereunto, and abjure all allegiance and fidelity to the United States of America. Gary's life journey that led him up to that point was pretty unexpected.
这一切发生的同时，他还在与那些完全不知道该拿他怎么办的官僚们对抗。这一系列事件的导火索，是加里决定站在巴黎美国大使馆一个穿灰西装的家伙面前，将手放在《圣经》上，宣读纸上的一两行字。我在此宣誓，我希望正式放弃我的美国国籍，并因此绝对且彻底放弃我在美国的国籍以及与之相关的一切权利和特权，并放弃对美利坚合众国的一切忠诚和效忠。导致加里走到这一步的人生旅程是相当出人意料的。

8
00:04:32.000 --> 00:05:06.000
You see, growing up in the 1930s and 40s, he had a lot going for him. He was born into privilege to a wealthy Philadelphia family whose car was a chauffeur at Rolls-Royce. His father, Meyer Davis, was a famous bandleader at the time. This is his orchestra you're hearing right now. Family friends included Ethel Merman and Bob Hope. As the young man, Gary was a rising Broadway star who once performed as an understudy to the famous actor Danny Kay. Then came World War II.
你看，在 20 世纪 30 和 40 年代成长期间，他可谓占尽天时地利。他出生于费城的一个富裕家庭，家境优越，他们的座驾是劳斯莱斯专车司机。他的父亲迈尔·戴维斯（Meyer Davis）当时是一位著名的乐队领队。你现在听到的就是他的管弦乐队。他的家庭朋友包括埃瑟·默曼（Ethel Merman）和鲍勃·霍普（Bob Hope）。作为一个年轻人，加里是百老汇冉冉升起的新星，曾担任著名演员丹尼·凯（Danny Kaye）的候补演员。然后第二次世界大战爆发了。

9
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:39.000
Gary was drafted into the Army Air Corps and trained to pilot a B-17 bomber. The experience had a profound impact on him. That was a whole reassessment of my role in life. How dared they put me in this role? How humiliating to my soul, my moral character, my profession. Everything you learn in kindergarten is being thrust aside and now you're a killer in the name of the nation. But you're not prepared for that. You're not prepared to even think about that. There's no education for that. At least it wasn't for me.
加里被征召加入陆军航空兵，并接受了驾驶 B-17 轰炸机的训练。这段经历对他的影响深远。那是对我在生活中的角色的一次彻底重新评估。他们怎么敢把我放在这个角色里？这对我灵魂、我的道德品质、我的职业是多么大的侮辱。你在幼儿园学到的一切都被抛在一边，现在你成了以国家名义杀人的杀手。但你对此毫无准备。你甚至没有准备好去思考这个问题。根本没有这方面的教育。至少对我来说是没有的。

10
00:05:39.000 --> 00:06:33.000
Flying bombing raids over Brandenburg and losing his older brother Bud to a German torpedo caused him to reevaluate not just his participation in the war, but his identity as an American citizen. I said there is something intrinsically wrong with society, and I'm not going to play this game anymore. So that started me on a whole wave of thinking about how not to play the game without going to a desert island, you know, and canceling out. He started reading about philosophy and history and studying the law. Eventually, he concluded that the only way to prevent future wars was for people to remove themselves from the system that creates the us versus them mentality. So after a year of planning and strategizing, Gary decided to act. I walked into the U.S. Embassy on May 25th, 1948, and I asked to renounce my nationality.
在勃兰登堡上空执行轰炸任务，以及哥哥巴德（Bud）死于德国鱼雷，使他不仅重新评估了自己对战争的参与，还重新评估了自己作为美国公民的身份。我说，社会本身存在根本性的错误，我再也不想玩这个游戏了。于是这引发了我的一系列思考：如何在不退居荒岛且不与世隔绝的情况下，做到不参与这个游戏。他开始阅读哲学和历史并研究法律。最终，他得出结论，防止未来战争的唯一方法是让人类脱离那种制造“我们对他们”心态的系统。因此，经过一年的规划和筹备，加里决定付诸行动。我在 1948 年 5 月 25 日走进了美国大使馆，要求放弃我的国籍。

11
00:06:33.000 --> 00:07:00.000
Well, everybody was appalled, of course. We're here to protect you. You know, I said, no, you can't protect me. You know, you didn't protect my brother. And you didn't protect anybody outside that I killed and so forth. So finally, I convinced them. The U.S. State Department says just a few thousand Americans a year give up their citizens. So Gary's action was kind of unusual to begin with. What made it even stranger was that he wasn't becoming a citizen of any other country.
当然，所有人都惊呆了。我们是来保护你的。你知道，我说，不，你们保护不了我。你知道，你们没有保护我的哥哥。你们也没有保护我在外面杀掉的任何人等等。所以最后，我说服了他们。美国国务院表示，每年只有几千名美国人放弃公民身份。因此加里的举动一开始就有点不同寻常。更奇怪的是，他并没有加入任何其他国家的国籍。

12
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:31.000
They said, okay, Mr. Davis, now you're a stateless person. And you have something that belongs to us. I said, what was that? The United States passport, please give it to us. So then the fun began. Gary left the embassy and went to the local office of the Associated Press to tell his story to the world. Then he went back to his hotel room to try to get some. some rest, but word had gotten out, so the phone began ringing constantly. First, it was United
他们说，好吧，戴维斯先生，现在你是个无国籍人士了。你手里有属于我们的东西。我说，那是什么？美国护照，请交给我们。于是好戏开场了。加里离开大使馆，来到美联社当地办公室，向世界讲述他的故事。然后他回到酒店房间试图休息一下，但消息已经传开了，电话开始响个不停。首先是合众社，

13
00:07:31.000 --> 00:08:05.000
Press, then Reuters, then the International News Service, all asking him for interviews. As I sat in Gary's living room listening to his stories, I asked him to show me his scrapbook of press clippings from the days that followed. These are U.S. stories. The Lordafia News, May 27, 1948. This went all over the press. What is this headline here? Say, young citizen of nowhere must live there, too. See, the journalists had a lot of fun with me. I mean, here was a big story. It was unique.
然后是路透社，接着是国际新闻社，都要求采访他。当我坐在加里的客厅里听他讲这些故事时，我请他给我看他珍藏的剪贴报，里面记录了接下来的日子的剪报。这些都是美国的报道。《洛德菲亚新闻》，1948 年 5 月 27 日。这篇报道登上了各大媒体。这里的头条是什么？上面写着，无国籍的年轻公民也必须在那里生活。你看，记者们拿我开玩笑。我的意思是，这是一个大新闻。它是独一无二的。

14
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:26.000
It was personal. It was political. It was active. The guy was on the line himself. It wasn't just a theory. Did people see it as a publicity stunt? The publicity stunt didn't come into play simply because of the renunciation. This guy's giving up United States citizenship.
它是个人的。它是政治的。它是积极的。当事人亲自上阵了。这不仅仅是一个理论。人们把它看作是一场公关特技吗？公关特技并不是因为放弃国籍而产生的。这家伙居然放弃了美国公民身份，

15
00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:45.000
that's not a publicity stunt. It threw in a counterlight the so-called theory that everybody wanted to be a United States citizen. Everybody was trying to get into the United States after the war. Here's a guy who gives up his United States citizenship. What is he? Is a crackpot? Is he a clown? What's his motivation?
这可不是什么公关特技。它以一种对立的光芒打破了所谓“每个人都想成为美国公民”的理论。战后每个人都试图进入美国。这里却有一个人放弃了他的美国国籍。他是什么人？是个怪人？是个小丑？他的动机是什么？

16
00:08:45.000 --> 00:09:32.000
In other words, we want to find out more of who he is and what his real reasons are. Gary attempted to answer some of these questions by releasing a statement explaining his action. In the absence of an international government, our world politically is now a naked anarchy. Two global wars have shown that as long as two or more powerful, sovereign nation states regard their own national law supreme and sufficient to handle affairs between nations, there can be no order on a planetary level. This international anarchy is moving swiftly toward a final war. I no longer find it compatible with my inner convictions to contribute to this anarchy,
换句话说，我们想更多地了解他是谁以及他真正的动机是什么。加里试图通过发表一份解释其行为的声明来回答其中一些问题。在没有国际政府的情况下，我们在政治上的世界现在是一个赤裸裸的无政府状态。两次全球大战表明，只要两个或更多强大的主权民族国家仍然视其自身的国家法律为至高无上、足以处理国家间事务，那么在星球层面上就不可能有秩序可言。这种国际上的无政府状态正迅速走向终极战争。我不再发现这符合我内心的信念，去助长这种无政府状态，

17
00:09:32.000 --> 00:10:14.000
and thus be a party to the inevitable annihilation of our civilization by remaining solely loyal to one of these sovereign nation states. I should like to consider myself a citizen of the world. To be clear, this wasn't an entirely new or original concept. Many notable individuals throughout history, including Socrates, Charlie Chaplin, and more recently Barack Obama have called themselves world citizens, but Gary took this idea to another level. For the rest of his life, he'd be a free agent operating by his own set of rules and without any sort of roadmap or instruction manual.
从而通过仅忠于这些主权民族国家之一，成为导致我们文明不可避免毁灭的同谋。我想把自己视为世界公民。需要澄清的是，这并不是一个全新或原创的概念。历史上许多杰出人物，包括苏格拉底、查理·卓别林，以及最近的贝拉克·奥巴马，都曾自称世界公民，但加里将这个想法推向了另一个高度。在余下的生命中，他将成为一个自由人，按照自己的一套规则行事，没有任何路线图或操作手册。

18
00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:42.000
Being neutral, my position was outside the framework of the laws of the nations. It was very exciting, you know, wondering what's going to happen now, what's the French government going to say? The answer came quickly. Given that he was now undocumented, French officials gave him a deadline to get out. But without a passport, where could he go? Traveling anywhere was sure to be difficult. But just as Gary was considering his options, he had a stroke of luck.
保持中立，我的立场在国家法律框架之外。这非常令人兴奋，你知道，想知道接下来会发生什么，法国政府会怎么说？答案很快就来了。鉴于他现在没有证件，法国官员给他设定了离境期限。但没有护照，他能去哪里？去任何地方肯定都困难重重。但正当加里在权衡选择时，他交了好运。

19
00:10:42.000 --> 00:11:26.000
The day that I was supposed to be out of France was September 11, 1948. That was the day that the General Assembly of the United Nations took over a little part of Paris. at the Trocadero, and it was declared international territory. It was the only place I could go, actually, without going to jail or without going to another nation and facing the same problem that I faced in France. So I went to the United Nations. I made a press statement saying, I'm your first citizen, you know, I have to live here. Gary and some friends camped out on the front steps, calling for world citizenship as a path to lasting peace,
我本该离开法国的日子是 1948 年 9 月 11 日。那一天，联合国大会接管了特罗卡德罗（Trocadero）的一小部分巴黎领土，并宣布其为国际领土。实际上，这是我唯一能去的地方，既不用坐牢，也不用去另一个国家并面临我在法国遇到过的同样的问题。所以我去了联合国。我发表了一份新闻声明，说我是你们的第一公民，你知道，我必须住在这里。加里和一些朋友露营在前台台阶上，呼吁将世界公民身份作为通往持久和平的途径，

20
00:11:26.000 --> 00:12:08.000
something the UN's predecessor, League of Nations, had failed to uphold. Officials didn't know quite what to do with them. Then that November, Gary managed to gain access to one of the General Assembly's meetings, and he used the opportunity to draw attention to his cause. An incident that reflects the mood of an impatient world interrupts proceedings at the United Nations Assembly. All the press was there, and we had huge lights, and we had the balcony box wired up so that it could take our speech. All the delegates were on the floor. Eleanor Roosevelt, you know, with their headphones,
这是联合国的上一任机构国际联盟未能维护的东西。官员们不知道该拿他们怎么办。接着在那年 11 月，加里设法进入了联合国大会的一次会议，并利用这个机会引起人们对他的事业的关注。一个反映不耐烦世界情绪的事件打断了联合国大会的议程。所有的媒体都在那里，我们有巨大的灯光，我们把阳台包厢接上了线，以便可以录下我们的演讲。所有的代表都在大厅里。埃莉诺·罗斯福，你知道，戴着耳机，

21
00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:26.000
they were wondering where this voice is coming from. Mr. Bershinsky looks on as the interrupter, Gary Davis, self-styled first citizen of the world, is hustled out. Speak to the people, shall I speak? Can you let me? I said, I interrupted the name of people, not represented here. The nations you represent, divide us, separate us,
他们想知道这个声音是从哪里来的。别尔申斯基先生看着打断会议的加里·戴维斯（自封的世界第一公民）被匆忙带出去。对人民说话，我该说吗？你能让我说吗？我说，我是以不在这里代表的人民的名义打断的。你们代表的国家分裂了我们，隔离了我们，

22
00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:48.000
and lead us to the Biss of World War III. What we need is one government for one world. And if you don't do it, step aside. We're going to do it ourselves. We had screaming headlines the next day. Those headlines were seen by people around the world. Authors and intellectuals like Richard Wright, Albert Camus, and even Albert Einstein spoke out on Davis's behalf,
并将我们引向第三次世界大战的深渊。我们需要一个世界一个政府。如果你不这么做，就请让开。我们要自己来。第二天我们上了轰动一时的头条新闻。世界各地的人们都看到了这些头条新闻。理查德·赖特、阿尔贝·加缪甚至阿尔伯特·爱因斯坦等作家和知识分子都为戴维斯发声，

23
00:12:48.000 --> 00:13:23.000
and he began receiving letters from supporters near and far. Remember, the war had just recently ended, and memories of the Nazi occupation and the Holocaust were still fresh in people's minds, especially there in Europe, so folks were eager to hear what Gary had to say. Less than two weeks after his storming of the UN, he gave a speech in a Paris auditorium, and 20,000 people showed up. Many of them expressed interest in his movement, so he got to work creating something they could actually join.
